var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Core","page":"API","text":"","title":"Core"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Interface","page":"API","text":"","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Flags","page":"API","text":"","title":"Flags"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Walks","page":"API","text":"","title":"Walks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Pathsets","page":"API","text":"","title":"Pathsets"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Element-types","page":"API","text":"Everything else is documented at the bottom of Extras.","title":"Element types"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.children","page":"API","text":"children(node)\n\nGets the children nodes in a Taproot DAG (if defined) of node. This function is meant to be overridden to access Taproot.jl functionality on your own types.\n\nBy default, children(node) will return an empty array, meaning Taproot.jl considers node to be a graph with a singular node (unless you overload this function.)\n\nOverriding this function\n\nSimply define Taproots.children(node::MyType) = ... Where the ... returns a vector of the things you want to traverse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.children"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.childtype","page":"API","text":"childtype(node)\n\nGets the eltype of the children nodes in a Taproot DAG (if defined) of node.  This function is automatic ones the children function is defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.childtype"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.childtypes","page":"API","text":"childtypes(node)\n\nGets a type which we believe is the union of all children types (recursively). Gives up after depth 3 and width 4, falling back to Any. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.childtypes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.data","page":"API","text":"data(x)\n\nGets the data of a single node in a Taproot DAG (if defined) of x. This function is optional (recommended) to override and can neaten up your life. This function separates the idea of data that a node holds versus the children of your taproot.\n\nBy default, data(x) will return x, meaning Taproots.jl considers x (i.e. the entire node) to be perfectly valid data in and of itself. However, this is sometimes dangerous, because editing x can destroy the link to x's children. Override it to get rid of this danger.\n\nWhatever you define data(x) to be needs to be consistent with `setdata!()\n\nOverriding this function\n\nSimply define Taproots.data(x::MyType) = ... Where the ... returns whatever data is not already included in the Taproots.children function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.data"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.setchildren!","page":"API","text":"setchildren!(node, children::Vector)::typeof(node)\n\nSets the children of your node. This is required for the following functionality:\n\nprune! (and prune variants)\ntapmap! (and tapmap variants)\ngraft!\nuproot\n\nShould return the entire node once completed.\n\nYou will need to define this in such a way that the following should do nothing:\n\nsetchildren!(node, children(node))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.setchildren!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.setdata!","page":"API","text":"setdata!(node, data)::typeof(node)\n\nSets the data of your node. Useful if you want to be able to tapmap! your taproot without messing up your children.\n\nYou need to keep this consistent with whatever you defined for Taproots.data. In other words, it should be the case that the following does nothing. setdata!(node, data(node))\n\nIt MUST return the entire node once done (and is allowed to simply return data without modifying the node in place).\n\nThis is required to be implemented for the following functionality:\n\ntapmap! (and tapmap and variants)\ngraft!\n\nExample\n\nTaproots.setdata!(node::MyDataStructure, data) = (node.data = data; node)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.setdata!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Taproots.NotImplementedError","page":"API","text":"NotImplementedError\n\nThrown when your node type is missing a method that Taproots needs, such as setchildren! or setdata!. The message names the method to define.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.NotImplementedError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.ischild","page":"API","text":"ischild(potential_child, parent)::Bool\n\nTells you whether potential_child is a child of parent. This function acts recursively and is not the same as doing potential_child ∈ children(parent). Time complexity of this is ~linear in the number of edges in your DAG underneath the parent, so use with care for large DAGs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.ischild"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.isparent","page":"API","text":"isparent(potential_parent, child)::Bool\n\nTells you whether potential_parent is a parent of child. This function acts recursively. Time complexity of this is ~linear in the number of edges in your DAG underneath the potential_parent, so use with care for large DAGs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.isparent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.isleaf","page":"API","text":"isleaf([children,] node; connector = (node, child) -> true)::Bool\n\nTells you whether this node is a leaf node (i.e. it has no children that connector connects to). Pass the same children and connector you iterate with and isleaf agrees with your iterator about which nodes are leaves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.isleaf"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.isbranch","page":"API","text":"isbranch([children,] node; connector = (node, child) -> true)::Bool\n\nTells you whether this node is a branch node (i.e. it has children that connector connects to).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.isbranch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.preorder","page":"API","text":"preorder([children], taproots...; kwargs...)\n\nArgs:\n\nchildren is an optional function taking in a taproot and returning an iterator of children taproots. If supplied, this iterator will not revert to using the Taproots.children function. \ntaproots are the root nodes we'd like to iterate through \n\nSupports kwargs include:\n\nconnector, this is a function which takes in the current node and a child node and if it resolves to true, you will visit that child node\npathset::Type{<:PathSet}, this is a selection of how many times to visit each node / edge. Options include: AllPaths, NoCycles, OncePerNode, OncePerEdge\neltype::Union{Type{<:Shoot}, Tuple}, this determines what output you want. Options include: Node, Level, Trace, or combinations such as (Node, Level, Trace)\nsizeguess::Int = 256, a guess of how many nodes the taproot holds, used to preallocate internal tracking structures\n\nThis creates a lazy iterator as a preorder depth-first search of your custom Taproot. In this iterator, parents are always iterated on before their children. Unlike usual preorder depth-first search, this one will iterate on rightmost children first before doing leftmost children.\n\nExample\n\nfor x in preorder(x)\n    print(x)\nend\n\ncollect(preorder(x)) <: Vector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.preorder"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.postorder","page":"API","text":"postorder([children], taproots...; kwargs...)\n\nArgs:\n\nchildren is an optional function taking in a taproot and returning an iterator of children taproots. If supplied, this iterator will not revert to using the Taproots.children function. \ntaproots are the root nodes we'd like to iterate through \n\nSupports kwargs include:\n\nconnector, this is a function which takes in the current node and a child node and if it resolves to true, you will visit that child node\npathset::Type{<:PathSet}, this is a selection of how many times to visit each node / edge. Options include: AllPaths, NoCycles, OncePerNode, OncePerEdge\neltype::Union{Type{<:Shoot}, Tuple}, this determines what output you want. Options include: Node, Level, Trace, or combinations such as (Node, Level, Trace)\nsizeguess::Int = 256, a guess of how many nodes the taproot holds, used to preallocate internal tracking structures\n\nThis creates a lazy iterator as a postorder depth-first search of your custom Taproot. In this iterator, children are always iterated on before their parents. Parents will immediately follow their children (so some (possibly irrelevant) children may not be iterated before the parent above them).\n\nUsage\n\nfor x in postorder(x)\n    print(x)\nend\n\ncollect(postorder(x)) <: Vector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.postorder"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.topdown","page":"API","text":"topdown([children], taproots...; kwargs...)\n\nArgs:\n\nchildren is an optional function taking in a taproot and returning an iterator of children taproots. If supplied, this iterator will not revert to using the Taproots.children function. \ntaproots are the root nodes we'd like to iterate through \n\nSupports kwargs include:\n\nconnector, this is a function which takes in the current node and a child node and if it resolves to true, you will visit that child node\npathset::Type{<:PathSet}, this is a selection of how many times to visit each node / edge. Options include: AllPaths, NoCycles, OncePerNode, OncePerEdge\neltype::Union{Type{<:Shoot}, Tuple}, this determines what output you want. Options include: Node, Level, Trace, or combinations such as (Node, Level, Trace)\nsizeguess::Int = 256, a guess of how many nodes the taproot holds, used to preallocate internal tracking structures\n\nThis creates a lazy iterator as a topdown (level-order) breadth-first search of your custom Taproot. The top parents are iterated first, and then the next level down, and so on. All parents are guaranteed to be iterated on before the leaves.\n\nUsage\n\nfor x in topdown(x)\n    print(x)\nend\n\ncollect(topdown(x)) <: Vector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.topdown"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.bottomup","page":"API","text":"bottomup([children], taproots...; kwargs...)\n\nArgs:\n\nchildren is an optional function taking in a taproot and returning an iterator of children taproots. If supplied, this iterator will not revert to using the Taproots.children function. \ntaproots are the root nodes we'd like to iterate through \n\nSupports kwargs include:\n\nconnector, this is a function which takes in the current node and a child node and if it resolves to true, you will visit that child node\npathset::Type{<:PathSet}, this is a selection of how many times to visit each node / edge. Options include: AllPaths, NoCycles, OncePerNode, OncePerEdge\neltype::Union{Type{<:Shoot}, Tuple}, this determines what output you want. Options include: Node, Level, Trace, or combinations such as (Node, Level, Trace)\nsizeguess::Int = 256, a guess of how many nodes the taproot holds, used to preallocate internal tracking structures\n\nThis creates an eager iterator as a bottomup (reverse level-order) breadth-first search of your custom Taproot. This is topological order. The bottom children are iterated first, and then the next layer up and so on. Leaves are not technically guaranteed to be iterated on first, before parents. While the other iterators are lazy, this one is eager. This iterator is, of course, not capable of running on taproots which are cyclical (and it will abandon the cycles automatically).\n\nUsage\n\nfor x in bottomup(x)\n    print(x)\nend\n\ncollect(bottomup(x)) <: Vector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.bottomup"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.leafbatches","page":"API","text":"leafbatches([children], taproots...; kwargs...)\n\nArgs:\n\nchildren is an optional function taking in a taproot and returning an iterator of children taproots. If supplied, this iterator will not revert to using the Taproots.children function. \ntaproots are the root nodes we'd like to iterate through \n\nSupports kwargs include:\n\nconnector, this is a function which takes in the current node and a child node and if it resolves to true, the node waits for that child. By default, a node waits for every child not yet in a batch\npathset::Type{<:PathSet}, this is a selection of how many times to visit each node / edge. Options include: AllPaths, NoCycles, OncePerNode, OncePerEdge\neltype::Union{Type{<:Shoot}, Tuple}, this determines what each batch holds. Options include: Node, Level, Trace, or combinations such as (Node, Level, Trace)\nsizeguess::Int = 256, a guess of how many nodes the taproot holds, used to preallocate internal tracking structures\n\nThis creates a live iterator of batches (vectors) of nodes. The first batch is leaves(taproots...; connector). Each later batch is worked out as you ask for it: it holds the waiting nodes which isleaf([children,] node; connector) now says are leaves. Only parents of nodes already batched wait, so each batch climbs at most one parent.\n\nSo the connector rules, and may change its answers as you go, for example once a child has been processed. When no waiting node is a leaf yet, the batch is empty, and empty batches keep coming until the connector changes its answers. The iterator only ends once nothing is left waiting, so do not collect it with a connector that never lets go.\n\nWith the default connector, batches are never empty: each holds the nodes whose children all sit in earlier batches. Flattening them gives exactly bottomup, so a node lands in the batch numbered by its height above the leaves.\n\nNothing within a batch depends on anything else in that batch, so each batch can be processed in parallel. Like bottomup, this abandons cycles (and everything above them).\n\nUsage\n\nfor batch in leafbatches(x; connector = (node, child) -> !isdone(child))\n    Threads.@threads for node in batch\n        process!(node)\n    end\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.leafbatches"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.leaves","page":"API","text":"leaves([children,] taproot; kwargs...)\n\nLike preorder([children,] taproot; kwargs...), but only yields the nodes for which isleaf([children,] node; connector) holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.leaves"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.branches","page":"API","text":"branches([children,] taproot; kwargs...)\n\nLike preorder([children,] taproot; kwargs...), but only yields the nodes for which isbranch([children,] node; connector) holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.branches"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.traces","page":"API","text":"traces([children,] taproot; kwargs...)\n\nShorthand for preorder(args...; kwargs..., eltype = Trace).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.traces"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Taproots.tracepairs","page":"API","text":"tracepairs([children,] taproot; kwargs...)\n\nShorthand for preorder(args...; kwargs..., eltype = (Trace, Node)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.tracepairs"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Taproots.AllPaths","page":"API","text":"AllPaths\n\nA pathset that follows every path, so a node reached by several paths is visited once per path. The fastest option, since nothing is tracked. Never finishes on a cyclic taproot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.AllPaths"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Taproots.NoCycles","page":"API","text":"NoCycles\n\nA pathset that follows every path but never revisits a node's own ancestors, so cycles are cut. Same as AllPaths on an acyclic taproot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.NoCycles"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Taproots.OncePerNode","page":"API","text":"OncePerNode\n\nThe default pathset: visits each node exactly once, however many paths lead to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.OncePerNode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Taproots.OncePerEdge","page":"API","text":"OncePerEdge\n\nA pathset that crosses each parent-child edge once, so a node is visited once per parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.OncePerEdge"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Taproots.Node","page":"API","text":"Node\n\nSelects nodes only when iterating. For use in iterator functions such preorder(taproot; eltype = Node)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.Node"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Taproots.Level","page":"API","text":"Level\n\nSelects levels only when iterating. For use in iterator functions such preorder(taproot; eltype = Level)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.Level"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Taproots.Trace","page":"API","text":"Trace\n\nSelects traces (tuples containing the indices of children nodes to get to this same position) only when iterating. For use in iterator functions such preorder(taproot; eltype = Trace)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.Trace"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Extras","page":"Extras","text":"The examples on this page use MyType from the Index page, with a messier taproot that mixes types:\n\nmy_data = MyType(\"The Root\", [\n\tMyType(\"Some other data\", []),\n\tMyType(\"I can nest data here\", [\n\t\tMyType(\"Such nested, much wow\", [])\n\t]),\n\t\"I don't even need to stick to one type ... but obviously a string has no children\",\n\tDict(\n\t\t:x => \"Taproots.children is already implemented on dicts\",\n\t\t:y => :(\"As well as \" * \" other types\")\n\t)\n])","title":"Extras"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Iterating","page":"Extras","text":"Every walk is a lazy iterator, so you can use it like any other, using map, reduce, and Iterators.filter. Obviously you can just collect that into a vector if you prefer (and you don't need the laziness benefits).\n\nmy_data_in_a_vec = my_data |> postorder |> collect\nprintln(my_data_in_a_vec)\n\n\nIterators.filter(x -> x isa String, leaves(my_data)) |> first # gives us the only string in the data structure ... \"I don't even need to stick to one type ... but obviously a string has no children\"\n\nPackages like this one are nice but you may be worried about interoperability with other (potentially more expansive) packages. So to make it all work out plainly, you can grab an adjacency matrix of your taproot whenever you want.\n\nadjacencymatrix(my_data) # gives us an adjacency matrix of 1s and 0s\n\nYou are now able to shove the structure of your DAG into another graph-specific utility. But don't be too hasty; check out the cool things Taproots.jl can do first!","title":"Iterating"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Finding-nodes","page":"Extras","text":"There are handy functions to help you find nodes as well! \n\nfindtrace(matcher, my_data) # gets you an index trace of the first node for which matcher(node) evaluates to true. \nfindtrace(child, my_data) # gets you an index trace of the first node which is equal to child. \nfindtraces(matcher, my_data) # gets you every index trace of the nodes for which matcher(node) evaluates to true.\nfindtraces(child, my_data) # gets you every index trace of the nodes for which node == child. \npluck(my_data, trace; default = nothing) # follows the index down to the pits to get you back whatever is in that index spot, otherwise return `default`","title":"Finding nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Modification-of-data-and-structure","page":"Extras","text":"Now there comes a time in every Taprootian's life when they yearn to modify their Taproots in place. And this is a dangerous topic because if you modify a Taproot in place, you might destroy the links to the children inadvertently. So for this purpose, you would need to add a few more functions. \n\nTaproots.data(node::MyType) = node.some_data # provide a way to get any auxiliary data (other than children) that your node might contain. Can simply return `node` or nothing at all. Doing this will unlock sinking your type to a Taproots.Taproot. \nTaproots.setdata!(node::MyType, data) = (node.some_data = data; node) # provide a way to set data in your node, if any. It can also do nothing. Once done, it must return `node`. This one as well as the next unlocks `tapmap` and variants.\nTaproots.setchildren!(node::MyType, children::Vector) = (node.children = children; node) # provide a way to set children in your node. Once done, it must return `node`. This one unlocks `prune` and variants. \n\ntip: Tip\nTaproots.data is an easy one to implement, but it's pretty useless unless you also implement the other functions. Taproots.setdata! is also easy to implement – because it doesn't matter if your type is mutable or immutable. You can modify it (and then return the node) if it's mutable. If immutable, just return a newly created node with modified data.Taproots.setchildren! can sometimes be a bit trickier to implement – it requires us to actually mutate the way we get children.Remember that at any stage you can check whether your Taproots.data and Taproots.setdata! work just by calling them on each other. E.g. setdata!(taproot, data(taproot)) needs to look the same as the original taproot.\n\nNow we get the good stuff. We can map every node's data while keeping the structure sparkly. \n\ntapmap(x -> x isa String ? uppercase(x) : x, my_data) \n\nThis gives us something like this: \n\nMyType(\"THE ROOT\", [\n\tMyType(\"SOME OTHER DATA\", []),\n\tMyType(\"I CAN NEST DATA HERE\", [\n\t\tMyType(\"SUCH NESTED, MUCH WOW\", [])\n\t]),\n\t\"I DON'T EVEN NEED TO STICK TO ONE TYPE ... BUT OBVIOUSLY A STRING HAS NO CHILDREN\",\n\tDict(\n\t\t:x => \"TAPROOTS.CHILDREN IS ALREADY IMPLEMENTED ON DICTS\",\n\t\t:y => :(\"AS WELL AS \" * \" OTHER TYPES\")\n\t)\n])\n\nwarning: Warning\nx comes straight from Taproots.data(node). x itself is not a node, and as such the strings inside MyType also get converted. Very awesome, but be careful! If you prefer changing the entire nodes themselves, then: 1) ensure Taproots.data(node::MyType) = node; or 2) use the regular iterators such as postorder(node) which returns entire nodes.\n\nWe can also get rid of all the nodes we don't like (... except the root, topmost node. Prune won't get rid of that one, and for good reason.)\n\nprune(x -> x isa MyType, my_data) # keep only the nodes which are MyTypes\n\nThere are similar handy functions such as:\n\ntapmap!(f, taprootius) # modify all nodes in place\ntapmap(f, taprootius) # deepcopy and then modify all nodes in place\n\ntapmapif!(condition, f, taprootius) # modify nodes in place if they satisfy `condition`. Very handy if your tree has multiple different types. \ntapmapif(condition, f, taprootius) # deepcopy and then modify nodes in place if they satisfy `condition`. Very handy if your tree has multiple different types. \n\nleafmap!(f, taprootius) # modify all leaves in place\nleafmap(f, taprootius) # deepcopy and then modify all leaves in place\n\nbranchmap!(f, taprootius) # modify all branches in place\nbranchmap(f, taprootius) # deepcopy and then modify all branches in place\n\nprune!(f, taprootius) # get rid of children which do not satisfy f in place\nprune(f, taprootius) # deepcopy and then get rid of children which do not satisfy f in place\n\nleafprune!(f, taprootius) # get rid of leaves which do not satisfy f in place\nleafprune(f, taprootius) # deepcopy and then get rid of leaves which do not satisfy f in place\n\nbranchprune!(f, taprootius) # get rid of branches which do not satisfy f in place\nbranchprune(f, taprootius) # deepcopy and then get rid of branches which do not satisfy f in place\n\nYou also get other handy functions by implementing those functions:\n\ngraft!(taproot, trace, node) # Where `pluck` is get, this is set. This sets the trace to `node`. Only grafts that one trace, not other traces (which might lead to the same node)\nuproot!(root, child) # pluck out `child` and ALL its parents. Then reverse the directions of the arrows. This is the only way to reverse arrows in `Taproots.jl`\n\ntip: Tip\nMy suggestion would be to try create a Base.copy implementation for your type if it's not obvious and not simply constructed from a constructor of its fields.  We use Base.copy if it's available when doing all the non-modifying variants of the functions above. ","title":"Modification of data and structure"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Built-in-taproots","page":"Extras","text":"It's not the point of this package, but there is a minimal (but fully functional) mutable struct called Taproot which this package exports. You can use this to store data if you couldn't be bothered with nesting things in your own struct. Taproots, however, don't allow arbitrary children types.\n\nTaproot(data::Any, children::Vector{Taproot})\n\nAlternatively, you can convert your type to a Taproots.Taproot. \n\ntaprootius = tapin(my_data)\n\nYou can get back out again simply by providing some kind of sink. \n\nback_to_the_future = tapout((data, children) -> MyType(data, children), taprootius)\n\nIn the example above, the children will automatically be coverted to MyType because the sink function we provided gets called recursively. \n\nTaproots.jl also treats many base types like Dict, Vector, and Expr as taproots, and all other types as leaves. So you can do something like this \n\ndict = Dict(\n\t\t:a => Dict(\n\t\t\t:b => \"nonsense_data\",\n\t\t\t:c => \"more_data\",\n\t\t),\n\t\t:d => \"final_data\"\n\t)\nleafmap!(uppercase, dict)\n\nHere's another usecase: metaprogramming. \n\nnums = 1:100\nexpr = :(append!(map(x -> sqrt(x) == floor(sqrt(x)), nums), map(x -> x^(1/3) == floor(x^(1/3)), nums)) |> unique!)\nleafmap!(x -> x == :map ? :filter : x, expr)\neval(expr)\n\nThis last bit here is absolute raw power. Code expressions are abstract syntax trees, and so they are taproots as well.\n\nYou might be wondering at this stage, \"Is every struct actually a taproot\". Of course they are ... \n\nstruct FourLeggedStruct \n\ta\n\tb\n\tc\n\td\nend \n\nCan we just define the children to be (a,b,c,d)? Yes, we can, and it's easy. But sometimes it's better not to think of them that way, and that's why this isn't the default behaviour of Taproots.jl. But should you want to, you can just do this \n\nTaproots.children(x::FourLeggedStruct) = eachfield(x)\n\nOr if you just want to traverse a ton of stuff:\n\nTaproots.children(x) = eachfield(x) # now everything will do this by default (unless more specifically defined). \n\nThere is also one more thing this package does which I think it pretty handy and which is related to the main concepts that Taproot deals with, but does not actually a require a taproot. It requires nested data with keys. \n\ndict = Dict(1 => Dict(:a => Dict(1 => Dict(:a => \"Finally here\"))))\ngetatkeys(dict, (1,:a,1,:a)) == \"Finally here\"\nsetatkeys!(dict, (1,:a,1,:a), \"Not finally here anymore!\")","title":"Built-in taproots"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Seeing-the-structure-of-your-DAGs","page":"Extras","text":"This package is interoperable with Term.jl for a CLI view and adds two very nice graph recipes to Plots.jl.   The data inside these can get cluttered, so it's recommended that you just overload Base.show for your custom types.","title":"Seeing the structure of your DAGs"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Terminal-visualisation","page":"Extras","text":"@sprout MyType # sets up AbstractTrees.jl and Term.jl so that you can call bloom if you want\n@bloom my_data # shows the data nicely","title":"Terminal visualisation"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Plotting","page":"Extras","text":"using Plots, GraphRecipes\n\nplotdag(my_data)   # each node once, in layers so every arrow points down\nplotdag(A, B, C)   # several roots at once\nplottree(my_data)  # shared nodes are repeated once per path\n\nAny GraphRecipes.graphplot keyword (nodecolor, edgecolor, names, ...) passes through. The images in these docs are made this way: see docs/figures/make_figures.jl.","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#FAQ","page":"Extras","text":"","title":"FAQ"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Why-do-I-need-to-define-children-and-data-and-setchildren!-and-setdata!.-Why-not-just-children-and-setchildren!?","page":"Extras","text":"So that you don't accidentally lose the children by calling tapmap!, and you don't accidentally modify data with prune!.","title":"Why do I need to define children and data and setchildren! and setdata!. Why not just children and setchildren!?"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#How-can-I-improve-the-speed-of-tapmap!-(and-similar)?","page":"Extras","text":"Use tapmapif!(condition, f, taproot). This will only execute f and set data and so on if the condition is satisfied. ","title":"How can I improve the speed of tapmap! (and similar)?"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#What-if-my-struct-is-immutable?","page":"Extras","text":"If your Taproots.data is immutable, that's fine. You just need Taproots.setdata! to return the entire node (as always). I catered for that in tapmap! because this is so common with leaf nodes.  tapmap! will automatically reconstruct the children in a nice way for you – but, warning, it calls Taproots.setchildren!.  If your children are immutable, I haven't catered for that nicely, and neither tapmap! nor prune! (and variants) will work. ","title":"What if my struct is immutable?"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#How-do-I-do-this-kind-of-thing-for-my-filesystem?","page":"Extras","text":"You can find every single file (recursively) in a folder very quickly! Handy for including files.\n\nusing FilePathsBase\n\np = p\"path/to/folder\"\nTaproots.children(path::AbstractPath) = isdir(path) ? joinpath.(path, readdir(path)) : []\nleaves(p) .|> println","title":"How do I do this kind of thing for my filesystem?"},{"category":"section","location":"extras/#Reference","page":"Extras","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"module","location":"extras/#Taproots","page":"Extras","text":"Taproots is a library for traversing nested data structures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.AbstractSprout","page":"Extras","text":"AbstractSprout\n\nA node held by a walk, together with its level. Sprout is the traceless variant; TracedSprout additionally carries the path of child indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.AbstractSprout"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.BottomupFrontier","page":"Extras","text":"BottomupFrontier\n\nHolds every sprout of the taproot in preorder. A sprout is either one unique path from the root, or (when walking OncePerNode) a whole node. The queue works purely on indices into states. Each sprout keeps a linked list of edges up to its parents: firstparent[k] is its first edge (0 for none), edgeparent[e] is where edge e leads and nextedge[e] is the next edge in the same list. pending[k] counts how many of sprout k's connected children have not yet been taken; a sprout enters the queue exactly once, when its count reaches zero (Kahn's algorithm). Cycles never reach zero, so they are abandoned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.BottomupFrontier"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.Frontier","page":"Extras","text":"Frontier\n\nAn incremental way of getting the next shoots of a walk, with one frontier per WalkOrder. Each frontier is parametrised by the concrete shoot type it stores, so walks stay type stable, and implements:\n\nput!(frontier, shoot): queue shoot so that it will be seen next\ntake!(frontier): pull the next shoot out of the queue\npeek(frontier): see what is next without removing it\nisempty(frontier): whether anything is left\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.Frontier"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.LiveFrontier","page":"Extras","text":"LiveFrontier\n\nHolds the same sprouts as BottomupFrontier, but asks the connector which of them are leaves at every batch, so the connector may change its answers between batches. candidates starts as the leaves. After that, only parents of taken sprouts become candidates, so a walk climbs at most one parent per batch. readybatch! moves the candidates which are now leaves into ready, for take! to hand out. The rest stay candidates. reached[k] makes sure sprout k only becomes a candidate once, so it is taken at most once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.LiveFrontier"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.MultiTaproot","page":"Extras","text":"MultiTaproot(children)\n\nA synthetic parent of several taproots, so that walking it visits all of their nodes in the correct order, even bottomup and topdown. It is never yielded itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.MultiTaproot"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.PathSet","page":"Extras","text":"PathSet\n\nDecides which paths a walk may follow (all paths, no cycles, once per edge, once per node) by tracking what has been visited. Each pathset implements:\n\ninitpathset(::Type{<:PathSet}[, ::Type{N}]; sizeguess): create a tracker, optionally with storage typed for node type N and preallocated for about sizeguess nodes\ntracknode!(pathset, node, level): record node so that it is not visited again\nvisitnode(pathset, node, level): whether to visit node\nvisitchild(pathset, node, child): whether to follow the edge to child\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.PathSet"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.Shoot","page":"Extras","text":"Shoot\n\nA selector when iterating. For use in iterator functions such preorder(taproot; eltype = (Node, Level, Trace))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.Shoot"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.Taproot","page":"Extras","text":"A Taproot is a single node that that can contain other Taproots. It can itself be contained in a parent Taproot. It is not the main point of this package, but provides a convenience to persist structure when doing maps or other transformations. It is also useful if you simply wish to build a data structure which already obeys everything in Taproots.jl.\n\nYou can construct a Taproot either by:\n\nTaproot(data, children) where data is anything that you'd like to store in this node, and children is an iterable list of children\nTaproot(node) where node is some value that has Taproots.children and Taproots.data defined on it. This will recurvisely sink your object into a Taproots.Taproot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.Taproot"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.Walk","page":"Extras","text":"Walk\n\nA lightweight coroutine. walk! runs a walk's loop until it hits @yield, which parks the current sprout and the yielded value in the Walk and returns. The next walk! call starts with @resume, which jumps straight back to just after that @yield, so the loop carries on exactly where it left off. Every field is concretely typed, so walks are type stable and yielding a tuple or struct never allocates.\n\nsprout: the sprout held at the last @yield, the only local still needed after a yield\nvalue: the last yielded value\nstate: FRESH, SUSPENDED or FINISHED\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.Walk"},{"category":"type","location":"extras/#Taproots.WholeSprout","page":"Extras","text":"WholeSprout(shoots)\n\nSelects the sprout itself, wrapping the shoots the caller asked for, so that the bottomup mapping pass can reuse the preorder walk.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.WholeSprout"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.adjacencymatrix-Tuple{Any}","page":"Extras","text":"adjacencymatrix(taproot)\n\nReturns an adjacency matrix for this taproot and all its children. If two nodes are equivalent they will only appear once here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.adjacencymatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.branchmap!-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"branchmap!(f::Function, taproot)\n\nModify the branches of a taproot in place without destroying links to children.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.branchmap!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.branchmap-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"branchmap(f::Function, taproot)\n\nDeepcopy a taproot and then modify its branches in place without destroying links to children.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.branchmap"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.branchprune!-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"branchprune!(f::Function, taproot)\n\nThis removes any leaves of the taproot who do not satisfy the criteria given by f in place. f evaluating to true will keep that leaf. This is similar to filter. However, you cannot prune the root of a taproot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.branchprune!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.branchprune-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"prune(f::Function, taproot)\n\nThis deepcopies the taproot and then removes any leaves who do not satisfy the criteria given by f in place. f evaluating to true will keep that leaf. This is similar to filter. However, you cannot prune the root of a taproot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.branchprune"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.eachfield-Tuple{Any}","page":"Extras","text":"eachfield(x)\n\nReturns a tuple of the values in each field in a struct. Nice for if you have a known number of children.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.eachfield"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.filterwalk-Tuple{Any, Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Extras","text":"filterwalk(keep, [children], taproots...; kwargs...)\n\nA preorder walk keeping only the nodes for which keep(children, node; connector) holds. The node rides along with the requested shoots, so any eltype works.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.filterwalk"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.findtrace-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"findtrace(f, parent)\n\nThis is a slower (~linear) algorithm to find the first value where f returns true. It returns a vector which can be used to index into a nested data struct. You can index into your nested data structure using pluck.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.findtrace"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.findtraces-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"findtraces(f, parent)\n\nThis is a slower (~linear) algorithm to find all values where f returns true. It returns a vector which can be used to index into a nested data struct. You can index into your nested data structure using pluck.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.findtraces"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.getatkeys-Tuple{Any, Vector}","page":"Extras","text":"getatkeys(indexable_struct, trace, default_value)\n\nThis is just a convenience function to index into a nest object (potentially not a taproot in any way) by some iterable value of keys.\n\nExample\n\ndict = Dict(1 => Dict(:a => Dict(1 => Dict(:a => \"Finally here\"))))\n\ngetatkeys(dict, (1,:a,1,:a)) == \"Finally here\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.getatkeys"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.graft!-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"graft!(parent, trace, value)\n\nThis sets the node which matches a trace. A trace is simply an iterable set of children indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.graft!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.leafmap!-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"leafmap!(f::Function, taproot)\n\nModify the leaves of a taproot in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.leafmap!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.leafmap-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"leafmap(f::Function, taproot)\n\nDeepcopy a taproot and then modify its leaves in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.leafmap"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.leafprune!-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"leafprune!(f::Function, taproot)\n\nThis removes any leaves of the taproot who do not satisfy the criteria given by f in place. f evaluating to true will keep that leaf. This is similar to filter. However, you cannot prune the root of a taproot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.leafprune!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.leafprune-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"prune(f::Function, taproot)\n\nThis deepcopies the taproot and then removes any leaves who do not satisfy the criteria given by f in place. f evaluating to true will keep that leaf. This is similar to filter. However, you cannot prune the root of a taproot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.leafprune"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.linkparents!-Tuple{Taproots.BottomupFrontier, Type{<:Taproots.PathSet}, Any, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"linkparents!(frontier::BottomupFrontier, pathset, children, connector)\n\nLinks each sprout to its parents and counts its pending children. When sprouts are paths, each has one parent: the latest sprout one level up, which preorder guarantees. Children on cycle edges never became sprouts, but still count towards pending. With OncePerNode, sprouts are whole nodes and each is linked to every parent it has.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.linkparents!"},{"category":"function","location":"extras/#Taproots.plotdag","page":"Extras","text":"plotdag(taproot; kwargs...)\n\nPlots taproot as a DAG: each node appears once, laid out in layers so every arrow points down. Requires using Plots, GraphRecipes. Any GraphRecipes.graphplot keyword can be passed through. Nodes are numbered in topdown(taproot) order, which is the order names, nodecolor and edgecolor (keyed by (parent, child) indices) follow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.plotdag"},{"category":"function","location":"extras/#Taproots.plotdag!","page":"Extras","text":"plotdag!(taproot; kwargs...)\n\nLike plotdag, but plots into the current plot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.plotdag!"},{"category":"function","location":"extras/#Taproots.plottree","page":"Extras","text":"plottree(taproot; kwargs...)\n\nPlots taproot as a tree: a node reached by several paths is drawn once per path. Requires using Plots, GraphRecipes. Any GraphRecipes.graphplot keyword can be passed through.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.plottree"},{"category":"function","location":"extras/#Taproots.plottree!","page":"Extras","text":"plottree!(taproot; kwargs...)\n\nLike plottree, but plots into the current plot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.plottree!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.pluck-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector}","page":"Extras","text":"pluck(parent, trace[, default = nothing])\n\nThis gets the node which matches a trace. A trace is simply an iterable set of children indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.pluck"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.prune!-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"prune!(f::Function, taproot)\n\nThis removes any children who do not satisfy the criteria given by f in place. f evaluating to true will keep that child. This is similar to filter. However, you cannot prune the root of a taproot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.prune!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.prune-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"prune(f::Function, taproot)\n\nThis deepcopies the taproot and then removes any children who do not satisfy the criteria given by f in place. f evaluating to true will keep that child. This is similar to filter. However, you cannot prune the root of a taproot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.prune"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.setatkeys!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"setatkeys!(indexable_struct, trace, value)\n\nThis is just a convenience function to index into a nest object (potentially not a taproot in any way) by some iterable value of keys.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.setatkeys!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.tapin-Tuple{Any}","page":"Extras","text":"tapin(node)\n\nThe standard way to access the recursive Taproot constructor. Requires Taproots.data and Taproots.children to work.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.tapin"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.tapmap!-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"tapmap!(f::Function, taproot)\n\nModify all the data of every node in a taproot in place. f only acts on the data in each node. This can handle mutable and immutable trees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.tapmap!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.tapmap-Tuple{Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"tapmap(f::Function, taproot)\n\nDeepcopy a taproot and then modify its nodes in place. f only acts on the data in each node. This can handle mutable and immutable trees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.tapmap"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.tapmapif!-Tuple{Function, Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"tapmapif!(condition::Function, f::Function, taproot)\n\nModifies nodes in place if the entire node satisfies condition. f only acts on the data in each node. This can handle mutable and immutable trees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.tapmapif!"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.tapmapif-Tuple{Function, Function, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"tapmapif(condition::Function, f::Function, taproot)\n\nDeepcopy a taproot and then modify its nodes in place if they satisfy condition. f only acts on the data in each node. This can handle mutable and immutable trees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.tapmapif"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.tapout-Tuple{Function, Taproot}","page":"Extras","text":"tapout(sink::Function, taproot::Taproot)\n\nThis allows you to transform a Taproots.Taproot struct back into one of your own structs. The sink function needs to be able to transform a single node of a Taproot into a node in your struct. The sink follows the following structure:     sink(data, children) which returns whatever type you want.\n\nAs a simple example, let's say we have\n\nstruct YourType\n    printable_name::String\n    id::UUID\n    data\n    children::Vector{YourType}\nend\nTaproots.children(x::YourType) = x.children\nTaproots.data(x::YourType) = (x.printable_name, x.id, x.data)\n\nThen you can convert that to a Taproots.Taproot as follows:\n\nyour_type = ... # nest your stuff here as much as you want.\nyour_taproot = tapin(your_type)\nyour_modified_type = tapout((data, children) -> YourType(data.printable_name, data.id, data.data, children), modified_taproot)\n\nIn case you have multiple types in your struct chain, you should take that into account in your sink. For example, you can do different things in case your Taproot has String type leaves.\n\nfunction sink(data, children)\n    if data isa String\n        return data\n    else\n        return YourType(data.printable_name, data.id, data.data, children)\n    end\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.tapout"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.treewalk-Tuple{Any}","page":"Extras","text":"treewalk(taproot)\n\nThe nodes and traces of taproot in topdown order. Shared nodes are repeated once per path, so the result is always a tree (cycles are cut).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.treewalk"},{"category":"method","location":"extras/#Taproots.uproot-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Extras","text":"uproot(parent, child)\n\nGets a child in a taproot, and slices it and all parents out of the taproot, then reverses directions of all the arrows. This is the only way to reverse arrows in Taproots.jl. This can be a bit slow for the moment for large taproots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.uproot"},{"category":"macro","location":"extras/#Taproots.@resume-Tuple{Any}","page":"Extras","text":"@resume walker\n\nThe first line of walk!(walker): carries on from the last @yield, if there was one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.@resume"},{"category":"macro","location":"extras/#Taproots.@yield-Tuple{Any}","page":"Extras","text":"@yield value\n\nInside walk!(walker, sink): hands value out of the walk, remembering sprout. The matching @resume jumps back to the label right after this. With a sink, the walk runs eagerly instead: value goes to sink and the loop carries on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Taproots.@yield"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Index","text":"<p align=\"center\">\n  <img src=\"assets/taproots.png\" width=\"150\">\n</p>\n<h1 align=\"center\">Taproots.jl</h1>\n\nThis library helps you traverse nested structs and data (including your own custom ones) in easy peasy ways!  This library is inspired by AbstractTrees.jl and provides an abstract interface which you can plug into any struct and get going (you can also skip the interface and provide a function on demand). \n\nFor the purposes of this package, we'll consider any struct a taproot if:\n\nIt has nested data which can be accessed from the struct in some way (whether by using keys or by actually holding that data – you'll provide the function to do that)\nThat nested data forms a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) (although this package can handle cycles)\n\nMany structures in the world form taproots:\n\nFile and folder structures (potentially including symlinks)\nNested data structures such as JSON, YAML, and so on. Nested Julia Dicts also do this.\nA response variable with all its causally-related covariates. \n\nThis package provides a few lightweight tools so you don't need to write custom recursions each time. \n\nWorth a quick note that a taproot in this package is not quite as general as a DAG – with the main difference being that a taproot has only one root node, and all other nodes eventually connect to that root node. This is a key feature of this package. Instead of creating an adjacency matrix from your data and then converting that to a DAG(matrix) and then writing preorder(DAG) ... you can just preorder(your_data) right off the bat.\n\nThat being said, this API can still handle multiple root nodes. You can simply call preorder(node1, node2, node3, ...) and it will iterate through all those nodes at the same time, and (by default) only visit the overlapping nodes once. \n\n(Image: Three root nodes sharing a web of nested nodes)","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Index","text":"In the Julia REPL, type ] and then add Taproots.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-to-use","page":"Index","text":"Turn your own struct into a taproot by telling Taproots how to find its children:\n\nusing Taproots\n\nmutable struct MyType \n\tsome_data\n\tchildren\nend\n\nTaproots.children(x::MyType) = x.children \nBase.show(io::IO, x::MyType) = print(io, x.some_data)\n\nTaproots.children is a function, so you don't have to store the children: you can look them up from a key or anywhere else.\n\nThat's it. Your struct now works with (almost) everything in Taproots.jl. \n\nLet's build a small DAG. F, G and H each have more than one parent:\n\nH = MyType(\"H\", []); E = MyType(\"E\", []); I = MyType(\"I\", [])\nF = MyType(\"F\", [H]); G = MyType(\"G\", [H, I])\nB = MyType(\"B\", [E, F]); C = MyType(\"C\", [F, G]); D = MyType(\"D\", [G])\nA = MyType(\"A\", [B, C, D])\n\n(Image: The example DAG: A points to B, C and D, and so on down to H and I)\n\nNow you might wish to traverse ALL that data in a lazy way:\n\nfor node in postorder(A)\n\tprintln(node)\nend\n\nOr just the things at the edges:\n\nfor node in leaves(A)\n\tprintln(node)\nend","title":"How to use"},{"category":"section","location":"#Walks","page":"Index","text":"Every walk is a lazy iterator, so map, Iterators.filter, first and collect all work.  In the animations, the amber path is the route from the root to the node being visited.","title":"Walks"},{"category":"section","location":"#Depth-first","page":"Index","text":"preorder(A)   # A D G I H C F B E   (parents before children)\npostorder(A)  # I H G D F C E B A   (children before parents)\n\n(Image: preorder and postorder walking the example DAG one node at a time)\n\nnote: Note\nDepth-first walks take the rightmost child first.","title":"Depth-first"},{"category":"section","location":"#Breadth-first","page":"Index","text":"topdown(A)   # A B C D E F G H I   (level by level, from the root)\nbottomup(A)  # I H E F G B C D A   (every child before its parents: a topological sort)\n\n(Image: topdown and bottomup walking the example DAG one node at a time)\n\nbottomup is the one eager walk: it maps the taproot before yielding anything.","title":"Breadth-first"},{"category":"section","location":"#Leaves-and-branches","page":"Index","text":"leaves(A)    # I H E          (nodes without children)\nbranches(A)  # A D G C F B    (nodes with children)\n\n(Image: leaves and branches of the example DAG highlighted)","title":"Leaves and branches"},{"category":"section","location":"#Leaf-batches","page":"Index","text":"leafbatches(A)  # [I, H, E], [F, G], [B, C, D], [A]\n\nEach batch only depends on earlier batches, so the nodes inside one batch can be processed in parallel.","title":"Leaf batches"},{"category":"section","location":"#Shaping-a-walk","page":"Index","text":"Every walk above takes the same keyword arguments.","title":"Shaping a walk"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-often-a-node-is-visited:-pathset","page":"Index","text":"preorder(A; pathset = OncePerNode)  # A D G I H C F B E              (the default)\npreorder(A; pathset = OncePerEdge)  # A D G I H C G F H B F E        (once per parent)\npreorder(A; pathset = NoCycles)     # A D G I H C G I H F H B F H E  (every path, cycles cut)\npreorder(A; pathset = AllPaths)     # A D G I H C G I H F H B F H E  (every path)\n\nOncePerNode: each node once, however many paths lead to it.\nOncePerEdge: each parent-child edge once.\nNoCycles: every path, but never back into a node's own ancestors.\nAllPaths: every path. The fastest, since nothing is tracked, but it never finishes on a cyclic taproot.","title":"How often a node is visited: pathset"},{"category":"section","location":"#Which-children-to-follow:-connector","page":"Index","text":"connector(node, child) decides whether the walk steps from node into child:\n\npreorder(A; connector = (node, child) -> child !== G)  # A D C F H B E\n\nisleaf, isbranch, leaves and branches respect the connector too.","title":"Which children to follow: connector"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-each-step-yields:-eltype","page":"Index","text":"Ask for Node (the default), Level (depth from the root), Trace (the child indices leading to the node), or a tuple of them:\n\npreorder(A; eltype = (Node, Level))  # (A, 0), (D, 1), (G, 2), (I, 3), ...\npreorder(A; eltype = Trace)          # (), (3,), (3, 1), (3, 1, 2), ...\n\ntraces(A) and tracepairs(A) are shorthands for preorder(A; eltype = Trace) and preorder(A; eltype = (Trace, Node)).","title":"What each step yields: eltype"},{"category":"section","location":"#Children-on-demand","page":"Index","text":"Pass a children function first to skip the interface entirely (or to override it for one walk):\n\npreorder(n -> reverse(n.children), A)  # A B E F H C G I D","title":"Children on demand"},{"category":"section","location":"#Several-roots","page":"Index","text":"preorder(B, D)  # D G I H B F E   (H is shared, but visited once)","title":"Several roots"},{"category":"section","location":"#Interface","page":"Index","text":"Taproots.children is all you need to walk. A few more functions describe your nodes:\n\nchildtype(MyType)  # the eltype of children(::MyType)\nchildtypes(A)      # a guess at the union of every node type in the taproot\n\nischild(H, C)   # true: H is (recursively) a child of C\nisparent(D, E)  # false: E can't be reached from D\nisleaf(E)       # true: no children\nisbranch(A)     # true: has children\n\nTo modify taproots in place (with tapmap!, prune! and friends; see Extras), also define:\n\nTaproots.data(node::MyType) = node.some_data                              # the data a node holds, apart from its children\nTaproots.setdata!(node::MyType, data) = (node.some_data = data; node)    # must return the node\nTaproots.setchildren!(node::MyType, children) = (node.children = children; node)  # must return the node\n\nsetdata!(node, data(node)) and setchildren!(node, children(node)) should both leave node unchanged.\n\nSee Extras for modification, searching, built-in taproots and visualisation.","title":"Interface"}]
}
